y predominantly  caucasian  so i think i'm on the opposite spectrum  of what a fencer  looks like acts like and  is in person  i'm watching watson i'm a two-time  olympian and world champion  as a troubled nine and off-road i had to  do a lot of extracurricular activities  that was offered in my  middle school so i first picked up a  foil which is what they like to call a  sword  i just immediately fell off with it  i didn't think it would take me this far  but uh i've been rolling with it ever  since  people should know that fencing is  it's a combat sport they think it's  super  fluffy and all this but no it's really  intimate  you can literally hear them breathe you  can see their eyes you really see if  someone's scared if someone has no  emotions and that's so cool about the  sport is you can really gauge your  opponent emotionally which i don't think  you can in many sports  well i think the fencing community  they've embraced me but then you have  people obviously who are older who want  to see the sport the same  people have always said like you won't  win this competition you want make a  national team you won't  ever you know make an olympic team you  know you want free world champion i'm  gonna win just  make you eat your work  hate us your motivators it just comes  with territory  the first time i ever qualify for  olympics it's just the most incredible  feeling because all that hard work pays  off because  0.11 of the publisher goes to the  olympics  i think being outsider just you have  more drive than everyone else because  you want to prove you're wrong  i broke my neck  i beat cancer and i'm going to rio  this is the first time that women's  rugby will be in the olympics i'm  jillian potter and i play rugby for team  usa  rugby in particular is a very good life  lessons teacher so when you get tackled  you have to get back up no matter how  hard it is i'm going to get up  i broke my neck playing rugby in 2010.  but when i was hit  i felt and i heard